<p>Pillar 03</p>
<h2>Maintenance Reality: What Japandi Demands of You</h2>
<p>Upkeep rating:  3 / 5 — moderate, with two specific chores</p>
<p>Japandi is kinder than its serene photos suggest — but not effortless. Here is the honest ledger from homes we service.</p>
<p><strong>What works in your favour:</strong> super-matte laminates are the best fingerprint performers in our entire catalogue — a matte walnut shutter can go weeks without showing a hand mark where a high-gloss white shows one in a day. Linen is <em>supposed</em> to wrinkle; the style forgives it. And because surfaces carry few objects, the actual wiping takes minutes.</p>
<p><strong>The two real chores:</strong> first, dust on dark horizontal wood. In Bangalore's dust load, a black-stained console shows a visible film within about 48 hours — which is why we specify mid-dark tones like smoked oak for tabletops and reserve true black for vertical faces, where dust doesn't settle. Second, coaster discipline on veneer. Natural veneer under PU takes a water ring seriously; a ₹300 set of coasters protects a ₹40,000 tabletop.</p>
<p><strong>The aging story:</strong> at year one a well-built Japandi home looks unchanged. By year five, PU-sealed veneer has deepened slightly in tone — a patina the style was designed to celebrate — while linen softens and cane mellows to honey. What fails instead is the cheap imitation: foil-finish "wood look" membranes yellow at the edges by year three, and low-grade fabric pendants brittle under Bangalore's UV. Hard water is the quiet enemy in kitchens and vanities — matte black taps show mineral spotting fast, so we fit aerators and tell clients to wipe fixtures dry, not wet.</p>
