<h3>Where the money actually goes</h3>
<p>Japandi is expensive in exactly two places. <strong>Materials:</strong> natural veneer runs 2–2.5× the cost of laminate per square foot, dark-stained solid wood accents cost more than painted MDF equivalents, and handle-less hardware with push-to-open or profile channels adds 8–12% to every shutter it touches. <strong>Precision:</strong> minimal styles have nowhere to hide. A 2mm misaligned shadow gap that ornate styles would swallow is glaring on a bare Japandi wardrobe face — so fabrication tolerances tighten and installation hours rise.</p>
<p>It is <em>cheap</em>, though, in places that surprise clients: almost no false-ceiling drama (a simple peripheral drop or none at all), minimal accessorising budget, no wallpaper, no decorative mouldings, and fewer total units than a display-heavy home. A Japandi 3BHK often carries less woodwork square footage than a conventional one — the premium is per square foot, not in quantity. Going factory-direct removes the 20–30% showroom margin on precisely the veneers and hardware where this style spends, which is the single biggest lever on the final number.</p>
