<h3>Three execution details only a builder mentions</h3>
<p><strong>Grain direction is a drawing decision, not a carpenter's choice.</strong> On a Japandi wardrobe wall, every shutter's veneer grain must run the same way — we mark grain arrows on the cutting list, because one rotated panel is visible from across the room. <strong>Edge-band color is matched to the veneer's darkest tone, not its average</strong> — matched to the average, the edge reads as a pale outline around every shutter. And <strong>shadow gaps hold a 3mm tolerance</strong>: wider gaps collect dust visibly; tighter ones bind in monsoon humidity when ply moves. That humidity is also why we insist on the correct ply grade per zone — the difference is explained in our <a href="/compare/calibrated-vs-normal-plywood/">calibrated vs normal plywood guide</a>.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>The Indian substitution that saves lakhs</strong> Imported white/smoked oak veneer is the "authentic" Japandi surface — and the least necessary expense. Indian ash or hollock stained to a smoked-oak tone under matte PU is visually indistinguishable at two feet and saves 30–40% on veneer cost. Likewise, a super-matte laminate kitchen delivers 90% of the veneer look at less than half the price. We show clients both boards side by side and let their hands decide.</div>
